---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

/**
 * Corrections policy and accountability page.
 *
 * /about/ already states the editorial standards — start from what can be
 * checked, label the kind of work, don't fill gaps with confidence. This is the
 * other half: what happens when something published here turns out to be wrong,
 * and who is answerable for it. For a site that reports on unconfirmed claims and
 * keeps trackers current, a visible revision record is part of the method rather
 * than an afterthought.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Corrections | aiPressHQ"
  description="How aiPressHQ handles errors, how updated stories are marked, and how to report something that looks wrong."
>
  <section class="legal-page" aria-labelledby="legal-title">
    <header class="legal-header">
      <span class="label">aiPressHQ / Corrections</span>
      <h1 id="legal-title">Corrections</h1>
      <p class="legal-updated">Last updated August 10, 2026</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose legal-body">
      <p>
        This site reports on model launches, pricing claims, and rumours before they are settled.
        Some of that will turn out to be wrong. What matters is that it gets fixed visibly rather
        than quietly, so a story you read six months ago can still be checked against what is now
        known.
      </p>

      <h2>How errors are fixed</h2>
      <p>
        A factual error is corrected in the story itself, as soon as it is confirmed. The correction
        is not hidden in a rewrite: the article's <strong>Updated</strong> date changes, and a note at
        the point of the change says what was wrong and what it now says.
      </p>
      <p>The scale of the change decides the treatment:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Typos and broken links</strong> are fixed without a note. Nothing about the meaning
          of the story changes, so a note would be noise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A wrong fact</strong> — a price, a date, a model name, a benchmark figure — is corrected
          in place with a dated note saying what it previously said.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A wrong conclusion</strong> gets a note at the top of the story. If the argument no
          longer holds, that belongs where a reader sees it before reading on, not at the bottom.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A story that should not have run</strong> stays up, with an explanation of why it was
          wrong. Deleting it would break every link to it and hide the mistake.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Updates that are not corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Trackers and explainers are kept current on purpose. When a model's price changes or a
        product ships, the story is updated and its <strong>Updated</strong> date moves — that is the
        story working as intended, not an error being fixed. Where the distinction matters, the note says
        which of the two happened.
      </p>

      <h2>What is never done</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Changing a claim after publication without moving the Updated date.</li>
        <li>Removing a story to make an error disappear.</li>
        <li>
          Editing a quote or a figure attributed to a primary source. If the source itself changed,
          the story says so and links both versions.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections issued</h2>
      <p>Every correction made to a published story, newest first.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="corrections-feed" data-corrections-feed>
      <p class="corrections-status" data-corrections-status>Loading corrections…</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose legal-body">
      <h2>Reporting something that looks wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Use the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> — pick "Correction or source note" — or email
        <a href="mailto:hello@aipresshq.com">hello@aipresshq.com</a> with the story's URL and what you
        believe is incorrect. A link to a primary source is the fastest way to get it fixed: this site's
        whole method is to start from what can be checked, so a checkable correction can be acted on immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        Corrections are made whether or not the person reporting them wants credit, and reporting an
        error never requires an account or a subscription.
      </p>

      <h2>Who publishes aiPressHQ</h2>
      <p>
        aiPressHQ is written and edited by
        <a href="/authors/tejas-telkar/">Tejas Telkar</a>. Every story carries a byline, and the
        byline is a person who can be contacted about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The editorial method every story follows — what counts as a primary source, how
        interpretation is separated from evidence, and how unconfirmed claims are labelled — is
        documented on the <a href="/about/">method page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .corrections-feed {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin: 24px 0 40px;
  }

  .corrections-status {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .corrections-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .corrections-item {
    padding: 16px 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .corrections-item time {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .corrections-item-title {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .corrections-item-description {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initCorrectionsFeed } from '../scripts/corrections-feed';

  initCorrectionsFeed();
</script>
